---
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
import { EN } from "../../data/copy";

const model = `interface WorkspaceState {
  version: 1;
  active: string;                 // active tab id
  tabs: Tab[];
  pinned: string[] | null;        // null = every extension pinned
  installedExtensions?: string[];
  seededCore?: string[];
  extensions?: Record<string, unknown>; // state saved by each extension
  theme: "dark" | "light" | "transparent";
}

type Mode = "side" | "tiling" | "stack";

interface Tab {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  mode: Mode;
  tiles: Tile[];
  layouts: Partial<Record<Mode, unknown>>;
  focused?: string;
}

interface Tile {
  id: string;
  type: string;
  data: Record<string, string>;
}`;

const files = `~/Library/Application Support/fr.spark.desktop/
  spark.db               workspace, recovery snapshot, settings,
                         installed packages, registry sources,
                         file access tokens, agent activity
  spark.db-wal           SQLite write-ahead log
  spark.db-shm           SQLite shared memory
  extensions/<id>/<version>-<uuid>/
  run/mcp.sock           MCP socket
  run/tools.json         tool list cache for the bridge

~/Library/Caches/fr.spark.desktop/
  preview/<id>.bin       files downloaded for the preview
  web/text/<tile>.txt    page text extracted for an agent

~/Library/WebKit/fr.spark.desktop/
                         cookies and storage of the browser tile`;
---

<Docs
  copy={EN}
  title="Workspace"
  description="Tabs, tiles, the three layout modes, the journal, the brief and the files persisted on disk."
  slug="workspace"
>
  <p class="docs__lead">
    The workspace is a plain serialisable document. Everything the window shows is derived from
    it, and everything an agent reads is a bounded summary of it.
  </p>

  <h2>Model</h2>
  <pre><code>{model}</code></pre>
  <ul>
    <li><code>Tile.data</code> holds strings only, so the whole document stays serialisable and bounded.</li>
    <li><code>extensions</code> is keyed by extension id; it stores whatever the extension saved through <code>api.state.save()</code>.</li>
    <li>Installed packages and registry sources use separate tables from the workspace in <code>spark.db</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Three layout modes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Side</strong> — tiles next to each other.</li>
    <li><strong>Tiling</strong> — free dockview layout: drag a tab to an edge to split, to the centre to group, drag a separator to resize.</li>
    <li><strong>Stack</strong> — tiles stacked vertically.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Each tab keeps one layout per mode, so switching modes never recreates a tile — which matters,
    because a terminal session dies with its tile.
  </p>

  <h2>Tile lifecycle</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>prepare(data)</code> normalises the initial data when a tile is opened.</li>
    <li><code>dataKeys</code> restricts which keys the tile accepts.</li>
    <li><code>describe(tile, surface)</code> returns a one-line summary for the brief; it must not throw.</li>
    <li><code>canClose(tile, closing)</code> can refuse a close, for instance when a document is unsaved.</li>
    <li><code>close(tile)</code> releases what the tile holds: a session, a file, a listener.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Disabling an extension removes it from <code>installedExtensions</code> but leaves its tiles in
    the workspace; their view becomes unavailable. Re-enabling restores them with their data
    intact.
  </p>

  <h2>Journal</h2>
  <p>
    <code>src/core/journal.ts</code> keeps 512 entries in memory. Each entry carries
    <code>seq</code>, <code>at</code>, <code>source</code> (<code>human</code>,
    <code>agent</code> or <code>system</code>), a <code>kind</code>, and optionally
    <code>tabId</code>, <code>tileId</code> and <code>data</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The journal does not replace the state: it recounts changes. It is bounded by
    <code>summarize</code> — strings cut at 160 characters, depth 2 — so a log entry can never
    carry a document or a terminal output.
  </p>
  <p>An agent resumes from a sequence number with <code>spark_events_since</code>.</p>

  <h2>Brief</h2>
  <p>
    <code>src/core/brief.ts</code> builds a summary of a tab: its tiles, their titles, their
    <code>describe</code> lines, recent events and the other tabs. The whole text is capped at
    1,400 characters and each summary at 120. Noisy events such as
    <code>tile.focus</code> and <code>tab.reorder</code> are filtered out.
  </p>

  <h2>Persistence</h2>
  <pre><code>{files}</code></pre>
  <ul>
    <li>The workspace snapshot is written to <code>spark.db</code> after a 180&nbsp;ms debounce.</li>
    <li>Writes are serialised: an older write can never overwrite a newer one.</li>
    <li>If the snapshot format is invalid, Spark opens a fresh workspace and keeps the previous snapshot in <code>workspace_recovery</code> on the first save. If the database is unavailable, Spark reports an error instead.</li>
    <li>Closing the window waits for activity, workspace and settings writes.</li>
    <li>The pre-rename <code>fr.lab.desktop</code> folder is adopted on first launch without overwriting existing files.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Undo</h2>
  <p>
    <kbd>⌘Z</kbd> and <kbd>⇧⌘Z</kbd> undo and redo layout operations: moves and resizes, including
    the ones an agent performed through <code>spark_tile_move</code> and
    <code>spark_tile_resize</code>. History is per tab and per mode, and never touches tile
    content.
  </p>
</Docs>
